# ================= new blocks
abstract = ("Chicago’s aldermen have long brokered city services, and requests with an aldermanic-office origin now make up roughly one in eleven resident requests to the city’s 311 system. Using 3.1 million completed resident requests from November 2019 to December 2024, matched to census tracts and to the ward map in force on each request’s creation date, I ask who uses this channel, whether it narrows the racial gap in service speed documented in companion studies, what the narrowing reflects, and whether the channel’s speed depends on the alderman’s electoral position or alignment with the mayor. "
  "Use is highly uneven across wards (0.7 to 24 percent of requests) and is lower in Blacker areas, largely because of differences between wards. The Black-share gradient in completion time is 0.141 log points per standard deviation for phone and for web requests but 0.031 for aldermanic requests. The difference survives every check I run, including dropping the highest-use wards and setting aside phone requests that are completed almost instantly, and it is clearest in Streets and Sanitation. What it reflects differs by department. In Streets and Sanitation, aldermanic requests are slower than phone requests from the same tract where few residents are Black and faster where most are; in Transportation they are far slower throughout. Aldermanic requests are less likely to be re-reported at the same address and no more likely to be left unresolved. "
  f"Using the Board of Election Commissioners’ canvass of the 2019 and 2023 runoffs with compiled first-round returns and endorsements, I find no consistent relationship between the channel’s speed relative to phone requests and seat safety, runoff contests, new office-holders, or alignment with the mayor, measured by endorsement of the eventual mayor and by the ward’s vote for the mayor; one of ten aldermanic estimates is significant at the 5 percent level. "
  "Because the channel carries about one request in eleven, and fewer in Blacker wards, its weaker gradient does little to narrow the citywide gap.")
ins = {}                                                  # insert after original block index
ins[16] = [P("Constituency service is a classic way to build a personal vote (Cain et al., 1987), and recent work uses 311 data to ask whether electoral accountability improves responsiveness to residents’ service requests (Christensen & Ejdemyr, 2020). If ward-office intermediation serves electoral ends, it should matter most where the alderman has something to gain or lose: in less safe seats, in contests that go to a runoff, or when a newcomer takes office and has no established relationships with department staff. It may also depend on the alderman’s alignment with the mayor; whether the alderman endorsed the eventual mayor in the runoff, and how strongly the ward voted for the mayor, are observable proxies. If instead brokerage is a routine administrative practice, these features should not matter.")]
ins[20] = [dict(t="hyp", text="<b>H4 (electoral position).</b> The aldermanic channel’s speed relative to the phone queue varies with the alderman’s electoral position: the safety of the seat, a runoff contest, and the presence of a new office-holder."),
           dict(t="hyp", text="<b>H5 (alignment).</b> It varies with the alderman’s alignment with the mayor: whether the alderman endorsed the eventual mayor, and how strongly the ward voted for the mayor.")]
